WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.990 --> 00:00:06.050
- [Narrator] Diagnostic
pelvic laparoscopy is used

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:08.750
to examine the internal
organs of the pelvis

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:11.773
in an effort to identify
a specific problem.

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:14.010
You might be advised

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:17.060
to undergo diagnostic pelvic laparoscopy

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:19.460
if you have pelvic pain or a mass,

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:21.980
an abnormal accumulation of fluid,

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:24.083
or difficulty becoming pregnant.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:29.030
These may be caused by endometriosis,

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:32.660
tubal pregnancy, an ovarian cyst or tumor,

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:36.950
scarring from pelvic
infections, or other conditions.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:41.870
Laparoscopic techniques require
only tiny keyhole incisions

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:43.250
or puncture wounds,

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:45.740
and the recovery period is much shorter

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:47.123
and more comfortable.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.470
When you arrive at the
hospital for your procedure,

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:53.710
an intravenous line will be started,

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:56.890
and you may be given a
sedative to help you relax.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:00.770
Pelvic laparoscopy is done
under general anesthesia,

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:01.900
which will put you to sleep

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.180
for the duration of the operation.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:06.770
A breathing tube will
be temporarily inserted

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:08.800
through your mouth and into your throat

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.520
to help you breathe during the operation.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.310
A catheter will also be placed

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:15.613
in your bladder to drain your urine.

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:21.630
An uncomplicated diagnostic
laparoscopy may take

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:24.740
as little as 30 to 60 minutes.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:28.530
Your surgeon will gain access
to the interior of your pelvis

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:31.430
by using a sharp
instrument called a trocar

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:34.360
to make a small 1/2-inch opening or port

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.913
just below your navel or umbilicus.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.470
Carbon dioxide gas will then be pumped

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.880
through this umbilical port
to puff up your abdomen

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:47.310
so its contents can be viewed more easily.

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:50.300
Next, your surgeon will
insert the laparoscope

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.230
through the umbilical port.

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:54.310
Images from its camera are projected

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:57.120
onto a video monitor
in the operating room.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.090
Your surgeon will carefully
examine your pelvic organs

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:02.440
and tissues, looking for signs of disease

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:04.730
that might explain your symptoms.

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:06.730
He or she may create other ports

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:08.990
through which instruments can be slipped.

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:10.910
These additional instruments are used

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:13.950
to move organs out of the
way for better viewing

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:16.450
or to perform operative procedures,

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:18.550
such as removing scar tissue,

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:22.630
taking biopsies, or
draining abnormal fluid.

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:25.670
When the laparoscope and
other instruments are removed,

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:29.210
a special gas valve is
left in place briefly

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:33.230
to allow all the carbon dioxide
to escape from the abdomen.

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:35.160
The keyhole incisions are closed

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.410
with just a few sutures or staples

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:39.323
and then covered with bandages.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:43.500
After your surgery, your
breathing tube will be removed,

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.370
and you will be monitored
in the recovery area.

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:49.530
Your catheter will be
removed shortly thereafter,

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:52.420
and you will be given
pain medication as needed.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:54.680
Your intravenous line will come out

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:56.570
when you can drink liquids.

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:58.300
Most patients are released

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:00.563
within a few hours of the procedure.

